<title>USA: U.S. Q1 nonfarm productivity rose 2.0 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Q1 nonfarm productivity rose 2.0 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-05-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>Labor estimates of U.S.</p>
<p>productivity/costs, seasonally adjusted annual percent changes</p>
<p>from prior quarters, on chain weights basis (1992=100):</p>
<p>    -	     - Productivity -	  - - -Output- - -</p>
<p>    -	     Q1-P   Q4-R   Q3	  Q1-P   Q4-R   Q3</p>
<p> NonFarm	   2.0   1.1  Unch	   6.8   4.9   1.9</p>
<p> Manufacturing   3.1   4.3   5.9	   6.7   4.3   5.0</p>
<p> Durable Goods   3.4   3.0   7.0	  10.3   2.8   6.0</p>
<p> NonDurables     3.5   5.6   4.6	   2.6   5.9   3.7</p>
<p> Total Business  2.1   1.2   0.3	   6.8   4.8   2.2</p>
<p>P-Preliminary. R-Revised.</p>
<p>    -	     - - -Hours- - -	Hourly Compensation</p>
<p>    -	     Q1-P   Q4-R   Q3	  Q1-P   Q4-R   Q3</p>
<p> NonFarm	   4.7   3.8   1.9	   4.7   3.6   3.3</p>
<p> Manufacturing   3.5  Unch  -0.9	   4.5   2.7   4.6</p>
<p> Durable Goods   6.6  -0.2  -0.9	   4.4   1.9   4.6</p>
<p> NonDurables    -0.8   0.3  -0.9	   4.0   4.1   4.7</p>
<p> Total Business  4.5   3.5   1.9	   4.5   3.7   3.8</p>
<p>P-Preliminary. R-Revised.</p>
<p>    -	 Real Hourly Compensation    Unit Labor Costs</p>
<p>    -	     Q1-P   Q4-R   Q3	  Q1-P   Q4-R   Q3</p>
<p> NonFarm	   2.3   0.2   0.7	   2.7   2.5   3.3</p>
<p> Manufacturing   2.0  -0.6   1.9	   1.4  -1.5  -1.2</p>
<p> Durable Goods   2.0  -1.4   1.9	   0.9  -1.1  -2.2</p>
<p> NonDurables     1.6   0.8   2.0	   0.5  -1.4   0.1</p>
<p> Total Business  2.0   0.3   1.1	   2.3   2.4   3.4</p>
<p>P-Preliminary. R-Revised</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast Q1 non-farm productivity to be up by 2.4 percent and unit labor costs to be up 1.0 percent.</p>
<p>Compared to the first quarter of 1996, first quarter 1997 non-farm productivity rose 0.9 percent, output rose 4.6 percent, hours rose 3.6 percent, hourly compensation increased 3.9 percent, real hourly compensation rose 0.9 percent and unit labor costs increased 2.9 percent.</p>
<p>The Q1 increase in non-farm productivity was the largest since a 2.8 percent gain in the fourth quarter of 1993.</p>
<p>The increases in both nominal and real compensation per hour in the first quarter were the biggest since Q1 1992, when nominal compensation rose 7.8 percent and real compensation rose 2.3 percent, department economists said.</p>
